# Cloudflare Pages custom headers.
#
# Root cause of the "Check for update" button saying "Already on the latest
# version" while the app was actually running an old build: sw.js had no
# explicit Cache-Control, so Cloudflare's edge (and/or the phone's own HTTP
# cache) was free to serve a stale copy of it. reg.update() only compares
# byte-for-byte against whatever sw.js it actually fetched — if that fetch
# itself came back stale, the browser correctly (from its own point of view)
# concludes there's nothing new, even though a newer sw.js is sitting on the
# origin. The service worker script must always be revalidated for the
# update check to mean anything at all.
#
# HTML gets the same treatment for the same reason — sw.js's own fetch
# handler is already network-first for navigations, but that only helps once
# the SW is running; a page load that skips the SW entirely (or an edge
# cache serving a stale HTML response) shouldn't be able to hand out an old
# index.html referencing old hashed asset filenames.
#
# Hashed JS/CSS (app.<hash>.js, app.<hash>.css) intentionally get NO entry
# here — they're immutable by filename (a content change always means a new
# hash), so long-lived caching for those is correct and desired.

/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate

/*.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate

/
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate

/manifest.webmanifest
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate
